    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost every garage floor you'd actually call good-looking isn't a single flat color — it's a base coat with flakes broadcast into it. The flake blend is doing most of the visual work, and it's also doing practical work most people never think about: hiding the dust, tire marks, and everyday grime that make a solid-color floor look tired within a few months. This guide is about that layer specifically — not which solid base color to pick (we cover that separately), but which flake blends pair with which base colors, how much of the floor should actually be covered in flakes, and how to choose based on how hard your garage actually gets used.
        </p>

        <div id="what-are-flakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What Color Flakes Actually Are</h2>
          <p>
            Decorative color flakes (sometimes called color chips) are small, irregular pieces of vinyl or acrylic material, usually somewhere between an eighth of an inch and half an inch across. During installation, once the pigmented epoxy base coat is rolled on and still wet, the installer broadcasts the flakes into it by hand or with a hopper gun — literally tossing or spraying them across the surface so they land and stick at random. After the base coat cures, any loose flakes get scraped and vacuumed off, and the whole floor is sealed under one or more coats of clear epoxy or polyaspartic topcoat, which locks the flakes in place, fills in the texture, and gives the floor its shine and chemical resistance.
          </p>
          <p>
            The result is the speckled, stone-like look you've almost certainly seen in a dealership showroom, a nice commercial garage, or a neighbor's recently redone floor. It reads as more expensive and more deliberate than a flat color, and there are real practical reasons it's become the default rather than just a style trend.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>It hides imperfections far better than a solid color.</strong> A single flat color shows every speck of dust, every tire mark, and every faint stain almost immediately — flat surfaces have nowhere for the eye to get distracted. A flake blend breaks up the surface visually, so dust, light scuffing, and minor blemishes blend into the pattern instead of standing out against a clean color field.</li>
            <li><strong>It adds texture and visual interest.</strong> A flat epoxy floor, however nice the color, can look a little sterile or plasticky at scale. Flakes add depth and a sense of material — closer to natural stone than to painted concrete.</li>
            <li><strong>It mimics a granite or terrazzo look for a fraction of the cost.</strong> Real stone or terrazzo flooring runs many times the price of an epoxy flake system. A well-chosen blend gets you 80% of that upscale look on a garage-floor budget.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            It's worth being clear about what flakes don't do: they're a cosmetic and camouflage layer, not a structural or waterproofing one. The clear topcoat sealing them in is what provides most of the chemical and abrasion resistance, so a cheap topcoat over a nice flake blend will still wear out fast, and a great topcoat over a boring blend will still look boring. Flakes and topcoat quality are separate decisions, and it's the combination of a well-chosen blend and a durable topcoat that produces a floor that both looks good and holds up.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you're still deciding on the base color itself rather than the flake blend on top of it, our <a href="/epoxy-floor-colors/">epoxy floor colors guide</a> covers solid base-color selection in depth. This guide picks up from there and focuses entirely on what happens once flakes enter the picture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="material" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Vinyl vs. Acrylic Flakes: Does It Matter?</h2>
          <p>
            Most DIY kits and professional installers use one of two flake materials, and the difference is worth knowing before you buy — though for most garages it's a minor consideration next to color and density.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Vinyl flakes</strong> are the more common, more affordable option, and what you'll find in the majority of DIY epoxy kits sold at home centers. They come in a wide range of pre-blended color mixes, hold their color well under a UV-stable topcoat, and are perfectly durable for a residential garage floor under normal use.</li>
            <li><strong>Acrylic flakes</strong> (sometimes marketed as "chips" with a slightly different finish) tend to have a bit more color depth and a marginally more polished look up close, and some professional installers prefer them for that reason. They typically cost somewhat more and the color selection can be narrower depending on the supplier.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            In practice, once flakes are broadcast, sealed under a clear topcoat, and viewed from standing height, the difference between the two materials is subtle — you're choosing a color and a density far more than you're choosing a material. If a kit or installer doesn't specify which one they use, it's not worth losing sleep over; ask about it only if you're chasing a very specific premium look and comparing quotes from multiple installers who use different flake suppliers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="density" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Flake Density & Coverage Options</h2>
          <p>
            Before you even get to color, the single biggest decision affecting how your floor looks — and how much it costs — is flake density: how much of the base coat's surface actually gets covered in flakes. This is a spectrum, but in practice most installers and DIY kits describe it in a few tiers.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Light / accent broadcast (roughly 10-30% coverage).</strong> Flakes are scattered sparingly, so most of the base color still shows through and the flakes read as an accent rather than a texture. This is the cheapest option in flake material and the fastest to apply, and it works well when you genuinely like the base color and just want to break it up a little. The tradeoff: it hides less dirt and fewer imperfections than heavier coverage, since so much bare base coat is still visible.</li>
            <li><strong>Medium broadcast (roughly 40-70% coverage).</strong> A noticeably speckled floor where flakes and base color are both clearly visible in roughly equal measure. This is a common middle-ground choice — enough texture to hide day-to-day grime and camouflage minor flaws, without using a full bag of flake per section and without fully hiding a base color you spent time choosing.</li>
            <li><strong>Full broadcast / "broadcast to rejection" (100% coverage).</strong> Flakes are applied continuously until the wet epoxy simply won't hold any more and stops absorbing them — this is the look on most professional showroom and dealership floors. The base coat all but disappears under the flakes, which means the finished color is really the flake blend's average tone rather than the base coat color itself. This tier hides the most dirt, tire marks, and imperfections by a wide margin, and gives the thickest, most durable-feeling texture underfoot. It also uses substantially more flake material — often several times the pounds-per-square-foot of a light broadcast — and takes an installer noticeably longer to apply evenly, so expect it to cost more in both materials and labor.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Density Changes the Color Outcome, Not Just the Texture</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">A light broadcast over a Storm Gray base still looks predominantly gray. A full broadcast of a black-white-gray blend over that same Storm Gray base looks like a mottled charcoal-and-white terrazzo — the base color barely registers. If you're picking a combination from a small physical sample chip, ask what density it represents, because the same blend name can look quite different at 20% versus 100% coverage.</p>
          </div>
          <p>
            For a heavily used garage — daily parking, a workbench, tools dropped on the floor — we lean toward medium-to-full broadcast almost every time. The extra flake cost is small relative to the total project, and the practical payoff (a floor that still looks clean after a summer of yard work and oil changes) is worth it. For a garage that's mostly a clean, low-traffic space, a lighter broadcast can look plenty good and saves some money.
          </p>
          <p>
            Density also affects how the floor feels underfoot and how it photographs. A full broadcast has a slightly grippier, more textured surface than a light one, since there's simply more flake material embedded under the topcoat — a minor but real benefit for a garage floor that gets walked on in work boots or wet shoes. It also tends to photograph better for resale listings and social media, since the eye reads a fully broadcast floor as more finished and more expensive than a sparse one, even when the underlying material cost difference is modest relative to the whole project.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="combinations" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Base + Flake Combinations We Rate</h2>
          <p>
            These are the pairings that come up again and again in garages that still look good years later, along with the color swatches to give you a rough sense of each blend. Treat the little dot patterns as a stand-in for "small flakes scattered over a base color" — actual flake shapes are irregular, but the color relationships are accurate. Every one of these is a real, commonly stocked combination you can ask for by description at most epoxy suppliers, even if the exact marketing name varies by brand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storm Gray Base + Black / White / Gray Flake Blend</h3>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 my-4">
            <div style="background-color:#6B7280; width:64px; height:64px; border-radius:8px; position:relative;">
              <div style="background-color:#1F2937; width:9px; height:9px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:8px; left:10px;"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#F3F4F6; width:8px; height:8px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:24px; left:34px;"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#374151; width:7px; height:7px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:40px; left:14px;"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#D1D5DB; width:8px; height:8px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:14px; left:42px;"></div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 m-0">Base: Storm Gray <span class="font-mono">#6B7280</span></p>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 m-0">Flakes: black, white, charcoal, light gray</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            This is the combination, full stop — the one that shows up in more garages than everything else on this list combined, and for good reason. It hides dust, dried mud, tire rubber marks, and small oil spots better than almost any other pairing, works with literally any garage decor or house exterior, and looks intentional at any flake density from medium to full. If you only want to make one decision and be done, this is the safe, correct one. Run it at medium-to-full broadcast for maximum forgiveness in a working garage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Slate Base + Cool-Tone Gray / Blue Flake Blend</h3>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 my-4">
            <div style="background-color:#475569; width:64px; height:64px; border-radius:8px; position:relative;">
              <div style="background-color:#1E90FF; width:8px; height:8px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:10px; left:12px;"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#CBD5E1; width:8px; height:8px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:26px; left:36px;"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#38BDF8; width:7px; height:7px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:42px; left:16px;"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#94A3B8; width:8px; height:8px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:16px; left:44px;"></div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 m-0">Base: Slate <span class="font-mono">#475569</span></p>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 m-0">Flakes: steel blue, sky blue, light gray, silver-gray</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            A cooler, slightly more modern cousin of the classic gray blend. The blue-gray undertone in the base and the touches of blue in the flake mix read as more "designed" and less utilitarian — this is the combination we'd point to for a garage that's meant to double as a showroom or a finished, styled space rather than a pure workshop. It still hides dirt well, just not quite as aggressively as the busier black-white-gray blend, since the color palette is narrower and cooler-toned.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tan / Desert Base + Earth-Tone Flake Blend</h3>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 my-4">
            <div style="background-color:#C2A878; width:64px; height:64px; border-radius:8px; position:relative;">
              <div style="background-color:#8B5E34; width:9px; height:9px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:9px; left:11px;"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#D9CBA3; width:8px; height:8px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:25px; left:38px;"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#6B4226; width:7px; height:7px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:41px; left:15px;"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#A9855F; width:8px; height:8px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:15px; left:44px;"></div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 m-0">Base: Tan / Desert <span class="font-mono">#C2A878</span></p>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 m-0">Flakes: dark brown, sand, espresso, camel</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Warm rather than cool, and genuinely useful if you're in a dusty, dry climate where the ground itself is closer to tan or brown than gray — think Southwest, high desert, or anywhere with a lot of red-dirt or caliche dust tracked in. A warm base and earth-tone flakes camouflage that specific kind of dust and dirt more effectively than a gray blend does, because the colors are already close to the dirt's own tone. It also just looks warmer and less clinical than a gray floor, which some owners prefer stylistically regardless of climate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Graphite Base + Metallic Silver Flake Accent</h3>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 my-4">
            <div style="background-color:#111827; width:64px; height:64px; border-radius:8px; position:relative;">
              <div style="background-color:#D1D5DB; width:8px; height:8px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:10px; left:12px; box-shadow:0 0 3px rgba(255,255,255,0.5);"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#9CA3AF; width:7px; height:7px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:28px; left:38px; box-shadow:0 0 3px rgba(255,255,255,0.4);"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#F3F4F6; width:6px; height:6px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:44px; left:16px; box-shadow:0 0 3px rgba(255,255,255,0.5);"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#E5E7EB; width:7px; height:7px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:18px; left:46px; box-shadow:0 0 3px rgba(255,255,255,0.4);"></div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 m-0">Base: Graphite <span class="font-mono">#111827</span></p>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 m-0">Flakes: silver, platinum (light or accent broadcast)</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            The dramatic option. A near-black base with a lighter, sparser scatter of silver flakes looks striking and genuinely "showroom" — this is the pairing that gets used in car-collector garages and high-end builds where the floor is meant to be looked at, not just parked on. Run it at a lighter broadcast so the silver flakes read as deliberate accents against the dark field rather than getting buried. The honest tradeoff: a very dark base shows dust, water spots, and light scuffing more readily than a mid-tone gray does, simply because there's less mid-tone texture to hide behind. Great for a clean, low-traffic, showpiece garage; less forgiving for a daily workshop floor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sandstone Base + Multi-Color Autumn-Tone Flake Blend</h3>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 my-4">
            <div style="background-color:#D8C6A3; width:64px; height:64px; border-radius:8px; position:relative;">
              <div style="background-color:#B5651D; width:8px; height:8px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:9px; left:12px;"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#8B5E34; width:7px; height:7px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:26px; left:38px;"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#C79A5D; width:8px; height:8px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:42px; left:16px;"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#6B4226; width:6px; height:6px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:16px; left:44px;"></div>
              <div style="background-color:#E8D5A8; width:7px; height:7px; border-radius:2px; position:absolute; top:32px; left:8px;"></div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 m-0">Base: Sandstone <span class="font-mono">#D8C6A3</span></p>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 m-0">Flakes: rust, walnut, gold, dark brown, cream</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            The busiest, most decorative blend on this list, built from five or more colors instead of two or three. It reads closer to natural stone or a terrazzo tile floor than any gray-based combination can, and it's a strong pick if the garage is genuinely a lived-in, decorated space — a converted workshop with a lounge corner, a garage that opens onto a finished patio — rather than a purely functional bay. Because it's already visually busy, it hides dirt and wear extremely well at any density above light broadcast, though it's a more specific style choice than the neutral grays and won't suit every house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Picking a Combination for Your Garage</h2>
          <p>
            The honest way to choose isn't "which color do I like best in a photo" — it's "how is this garage actually going to get used." Flake color and density both have real practical consequences, not just aesthetic ones.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Workshop or high-traffic daily garage:</strong> favor darker, busier blends — Storm Gray with black/white/gray, or Tan/Desert with earth tones if you're in a dusty climate — at medium-to-full broadcast. Busy, multi-tone patterns hide grease, dust, dropped tools, and tire marks far longer between cleanings than a lighter or more uniform blend does.</li>
            <li><strong>Showroom, display, or car-collector garage:</strong> a lighter or more monochrome look reads cleaner and more deliberate. The Slate blue-gray blend or the Graphite-with-silver-accent combination both photograph well and suit a garage that's meant to be shown off rather than worked in daily. Just budget for more frequent sweeping, since lighter/sparser blends hide less.</li>
            <li><strong>Mixed-use garage (parking plus occasional projects):</strong> the Storm Gray classic blend is the safest middle ground — it hides enough daily grime to stay looking clean, without looking purely industrial.</li>
            <li><strong>Matching an existing base color:</strong> if you've already settled on a base color from our <a href="/epoxy-floor-colors/">epoxy floor colors guide</a>, work backward — gray and slate bases pair best with the cool-tone or classic blends above, while tan and beige bases pair best with earth-tone or autumn blends. Avoid crossing a warm base with a cool flake blend (or vice versa); the mismatch tends to look muddy rather than intentional.</li>
            <li><strong>Planning to sell the house eventually:</strong> stick with the neutral, widely appealing options — Storm Gray or Slate blends. They read as a well-maintained garage to almost any buyer, whereas a bold autumn or metallic-accent floor is more of a personal statement that won't appeal to everyone touring the house.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            One more practical note: if you're not sure between two blends, err toward the busier, darker option rather than the cleaner, lighter one. In our experience, homeowners regret an under-camouflaged floor — one that shows every tire mark within a month — far more often than they regret a floor that hides slightly more than strictly necessary. You can always sweep and mop a busy floor to make it shine; you can't add more flakes to a floor that's already sealed under a topcoat without a fairly involved re-coat.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever you land on, order a physical sample board from your epoxy supplier before committing — screen colors and lighting shift flake tones noticeably, and a sample under your garage's actual lighting is worth more than any photo. For the actual mechanics of getting flakes down evenly, our <a href="/how-to-apply-garage-floor-epoxy/">how to apply garage floor epoxy</a> guide walks through the broadcast technique step by step, and our <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">best garage floor epoxy</a> roundup covers which kits come with flakes included versus sold separately.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Common Flake Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
          <p>
            Most flake regrets we hear about trace back to a small handful of avoidable mistakes made before the epoxy even goes down. A few to watch for:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Judging a blend from a tiny sample chip.</strong> A 2-inch color card can't show you what a blend looks like broadcast across 400 square feet. Where possible, look at photos of the actual blend applied to a full floor, or ask your supplier for a larger sample board, before committing.</li>
            <li><strong>Not asking what density the sample represents.</strong> As covered above, the same blend name looks quite different at 20% coverage versus full broadcast. A sample chip broadcast heavily can make you expect a look you won't get if your installer quotes a lighter application (or vice versa).</li>
            <li><strong>Mixing warm and cool tones across the base and flakes.</strong> A cool gray or slate base with warm tan or brown flakes (or the reverse) tends to look muddy and unintentional rather than blended. Keep the base and flake undertones in the same temperature family — both warm or both cool — unless you're deliberately going for a high-contrast, multi-tone autumn-style blend that's built around variety from the start.</li>
            <li><strong>Underestimating how much flake a full broadcast actually needs.</strong> DIY kits often include enough flake for a light-to-medium application, and homeowners aiming for a full "to rejection" look run short mid-project. Order extra flake material — most suppliers will let you return unused sealed bags — rather than stopping partway through and trying to match a discontinued color later.</li>
            <li><strong>Ignoring how the color will look under actual garage lighting.</strong> Garages are often lit with cooler LED shop lights rather than the warm bulbs common indoors, which can shift how a blend reads compared to how it looked in a showroom or in daylight. If possible, view a sample under lighting similar to what you'll actually have.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="metallic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. A Word on Metallic Epoxy</h2>
          <p>
            Worth a mention even though it's a different product category: metallic epoxy uses mica or metallic pigments stirred into the epoxy itself, applied with specific manipulation techniques (fans, chip brushes, compressed air) to create a moving, marbled, almost 3D lava-lamp effect rather than a scattered flake texture. It's genuinely striking in photos and in person, and it's the choice for a true showpiece floor.
          </p>
          <p>
            It's also pricier than a flake system, more dependent on installer skill (the swirling effect is hard to get right and hard to fix if it goes wrong), and generally less forgiving of daily wear — tire marks and scuffing show up more visibly against the smooth metallic sheen than they do against a textured flake surface. For most garages doing daily duty, a flake blend remains the more practical choice; metallic is worth considering specifically when the floor itself is meant to be the centerpiece and budget isn't the deciding factor. If you're weighing that tradeoff against a straightforward solid base color instead, that comparison lives in our <a href="/epoxy-floor-colors/">epoxy floor colors guide</a>, and if you're still comparing epoxy to paint entirely, see <a href="/best-garage-floor-paint/">best garage floor paint</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some installers also offer a hybrid: a metallic base coat with a light, sparse flake accent scattered on top, giving you some of the marbled metallic movement with a touch of the texture and dirt-hiding benefit that flakes provide on their own. It costs roughly what a straight metallic floor costs, but it's worth asking about if you like the metallic look but want a bit more forgiveness for everyday wear than pure metallic offers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are color flakes in epoxy garage floors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Color flakes (also called color chips) are small pieces of vinyl or acrylic material broadcast by hand into a wet epoxy base coat, then sealed under one or more coats of clear epoxy or polyaspartic topcoat. They create the speckled, terrazzo-like texture you see on most professionally finished garage floors, as opposed to a flat, single-color finish.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most popular garage floor epoxy color combination?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">A Storm Gray base coat with a black, white, and gray flake blend is by far the most popular combination. It hides dust, tire marks, oil residue, and minor imperfections better than almost any other option, works with any garage decor, and full broadcast coverage looks like a factory or showroom floor without needing a specific base slab condition.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much flake coverage do I need for a garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">It depends on the look and the budget. A light or accent broadcast (roughly 10-30% surface coverage) shows more of the base color with scattered flakes and costs less in materials and labor. A full broadcast (also called 100% coverage or "to rejection," where flakes are applied until the surface won't absorb any more) hides the base coat almost entirely, hides far more dirt and imperfections, and produces the thickest, most durable-looking texture, but uses significantly more flake material and takes longer to apply.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which epoxy flake color hides dirt best in a garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Busier, darker, multi-tone blends hide dirt and grime best. A gray-black-white blend over a Storm Gray or Slate base, or an earth-tone blend over a Tan/Desert base, camouflages dust, tire rubber marks, and light stains far more effectively than a lighter or more monochrome blend. If your garage doubles as a workshop, prioritize a busier blend over a cleaner, lighter one.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is metallic epoxy better than flake epoxy for a garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Neither is objectively better — they're different looks at different price points. Metallic epoxy uses pigments that create a moving, 3D, marbled effect and looks more dramatic, but it costs more, shows tire marks and scuffs more readily, and is less forgiving of an imperfect application. Flake epoxy is more practical for a daily-driver garage: it's more affordable, hides wear better, and is the more common choice unless you specifically want a showpiece floor.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Garage Floor Colour Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Colour is the part of an epoxy floor people spend the least time on and look at the most afterwards. A few errors come up again and again.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging Colour From a Chip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A two-inch sample tells you very little about how a colour reads across a whole garage in overhead light. Mid-greys look almost white on a floor, and warm beiges can look distinctly yellow. Look at installed floors in the same colour before deciding.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a Solid Colour in a Working Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solid colours show every crumb of dirt, tyre mark, and drip, and they highlight any imperfection in the slab underneath. Flake blends camouflage all of it, which is why they dominate residential installs. If the garage is used for anything other than parking, flake is the practical choice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Picking Flake Purely on Appearance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flake size and density change how the floor feels underfoot as well as how it looks. A heavy full-broadcast floor with a thick topcoat has real texture and grip; a light scatter is nearly smooth. Decide on traction first and colour blend second.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting About Yellowing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard epoxy ambers under UV, and it shows most on whites, light greys, and blues. In a garage where the door stands open, that shift is visible within a few years. A UV-stable polyaspartic topcoat is the fix, and it needs planning for at the colour stage rather than afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

